CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe autonomic panic response, including marked tachycardia (HR 110) and tachypnea (RR 24), alongside incapacitating diffuse somatic pain triggered by severe domestic psychological trauma. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was absolutely essential to perform a physical examination to rule out covert physical trauma, establish a safe and confidential clinical environment, and provide immediate pharmacological down-regulation of the nervous system. Delaying care until routine hours would have left the patient in an unmanaged state of severe panic and potential immediate danger, firmly justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral diazepam 5mg to rapidly reduce sympathetic overdrive and manage the severe somatic muscle tension. Conducted a brief, verbal safety planning session. Advised the usual GP to arrange for neuroendocrine evidence of chronic severe allostatic load and utilize the Danger Assessment scale at the next visit.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute hypervigilance safely, implement a "containment breathing technique": inhale slowly through the nose, hold for 3 seconds, and exhale forcefully through pursed lips; actively forcing the exhale engages the parasympathetic nervous system to physically lower the heart rate. To address the isolation caused by stereotype-reinforcement fear, agree to save one specific, culturally specific domestic violence hotline number in the phone under a disguised, safe name (e.g., "Grocery Delivery"); having the resource safely accessible provides a sense of control without risking immediate exposure.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Provided contact details for a culturally specific, confidential domestic violence support service.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 48 hours to assess physical symptoms, review safety planning, and provide ongoing confidential support.

